;; Reviewed 2026-10-06 (written by Claude, from the design, the MACRO
;; manual and the VAX's listing; for the user's review). Why this is right:
;; - An array is its elements one after another, aligned as its element
;;   (MemoryLayout, section 4): a module variable is .BLKB of its size.
;;   ARRAY 3, 4 OF INTEGER is three rows of 8 bytes.
;; - A constant index is a displacement: a[3] is A+6. The compiler has
;;   checked it, so there is no check at run time.
;; - Any other index is widened to a longword in a register of its own
;;   (CVTWL, CVTBL; a LONGINT is moved), then checked: CMPL Rx, #LEN-1
;;   and BLEQU over the trap, unsigned so a negative index fails too.
;;   The trap is POC_TRAP with code 2 and the index's line and column, as
;;   on LLVM (section 9). A HUGEINT index (a[q], line 40) also fails when
;;   its high longword is not zero (TSTL, BNEQ to the trap).
;; - An element of 1, 2 or 4 bytes is index mode, base[Rx], which scales
;;   Rx by the instruction's operand size (MACRO 5.3.13): a[i] := a[j] + 1
;;   is one ADDW3 of A[R1] into A[R0]. Index mode scales by the operand's
;;   size in the instruction, so an element used where the size differs
;;   is first moved to a register: f[i] for BLBS, whose operand is a
;;   longword (line 37); s[j] for BBC, whose base is a byte (line 38);
;;   a[i] as ASHL's count, a byte (line 39).
;; - A HUGEINT or an array element is scaled by MULL2 and its address
;;   made by MOVAB base[Rx], Rx, MOVAB's operand being a byte, so the
;;   element is (Rx), its longwords (R0) and 4(R0) (lines 33-34). m[i, j]
;;   is a row's address, then index mode in it, (R0)[R1]; m[1][j] is the
;;   constant row's displacement with an index, M+8[R0].
;; - The target's location is found before the value is evaluated, as on
;;   LLVM, and its registers kept while it is.
;; - c := a copies 20 bytes by MOVC3, which overwrites R0-R5, so the
;;   initializer's mask saves R2-R5.
;; - A VAR argument's address of an element is MOVAW, MOVA's operand being
;;   a word, so that index mode scales by 2: MOVAW A[R0] and MOVAW
;;   (R0)[R1].
